I've characterized the AMG (LLNL) benchmark, which builds a single executable `amg` (test/amg.c driver linked against the hypre-derived libraries: krylov, parcsr_ls, parcsr_mv, seq_mv, IJ_mv, utilities).

Key findings recorded for `amg:general`:

- **Build/subject**: Single SPMD executable `amg`, compiled via Makefile.include with `mpicc`, default flags `-DTIMER_USE_MPI -DHYPRE_USING_OPENMP -fopenmp -DHYPRE_HOPSCOTCH -DHYPRE_USING_PERSISTENT_COMM -DHYPRE_BIGINT`.
- **Parallelism**: hybrid MPI + OpenMP (`hypre_MPI_Init`, `#pragma omp parallel` throughout parcsr_mv/parcsr_ls); it's algebraic multigrid on a logical P×Q×R process grid.
- **Communication**: mixed collective + point-to-point, dominant pattern is neighbor exchange (`hypre_MPI_Isend/Irecv/Waitall` in par_csr_communication.c) for parallel matvec/halo-like ghost updates, plus `MPI_Allreduce`/`MPI_Bcast` for global setup info (nnz counts, partitioning). Docs explicitly call it "highly synchronous" with a "surface-to-volume" communication/computation pattern — latency-sensitive, not bandwidth-heavy.
- **Memory**: no shared-memory windows/mmap (`memory.shared_memory=false`); memory-access bound per docs, so NUMA/thread pinning matters (cpu_exclusive=true judgment) given production runs pin one thread per core (e.g. BG/Q 4 OpenMP threads/task in doc example).
- **Accelerator**: none — no CUDA/HIP/SYCL source anywhere in the tree; CPU-only code.
- **I/O**: none — driver synthesizes its own linear system in-memory (`AMG generates its own linear system`); no checkpoint/restart.
- **Launch geometry**: only literal example found is `mpirun -np 4 amg -P 2 2 1 -n 50 50 50 -printstats` with `OMP_NUM_THREADS=3`, recorded as tasks=4, threads_per_task=3, launcher=mpirun.
- **Topology**: docs describe scaling from 512 to 65,536+ MPI ranks (BG/Q up to >1,000,000), i.e. designed to span many nodes with neighbor-preferred placement due to the grid decomposition and point-to-point halo exchange.

Recorded as a single `general` variant since the MPI-only vs MPI+OpenMP vs BIGINT options are just compile-time flags on the same code path/subject, not materially different scheduling shapes, and there's no GPU/alternate build variant in this source tree.
